    {
      "text": "Network or resolution failures are often associated with gRPC status codes such as `UNAVAILABLE`, which indicates transport or connectivity issues like unreachable endpoints, connection failures, or name-resolution failures.",
      "sources": [
        "payment-runbook.md",
        "grpc-troubleshooting.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Specific diagnostic messages such as \"name resolver error\", \"produced zero addresses\", or resolver-oriented diagnostics strongly suggest that the client could not obtain a usable destination, pointing to resolution or network issues.",
      "sources": [
        "service-discovery.md",
        "payment-runbook.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "An application-level payment failure is distinguished by the presence of a server span, indicating the request reached PaymentService, where the failure occurs within the application logic (e.g., charge-processing errors, application logs, or gRPC server status).",
      "sources": [
        "payment-runbook.md",
        "payment-service.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Application-level failures may result in status codes such as `INTERNAL` (representing internal application failure or upstream translation of lower-level failures) or specific charge-processing errors, whereas network failures are primarily connectivity-oriented.",
      "sources": [
        "grpc-troubleshooting.md",
        "payment-service.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The causal path must be verified by checking if a failing client span reports a connectivity-oriented status with no matching server span (network/resolution failure) versus inspecting Payment logs and span attributes when the server span exists (application failure).",
      "sources": [
        "payment-service.md",
        "grpc-troubleshooting.md"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "limitation": null
}

